                  "content": "# Implementing a Reliable Task Queue\n\n## Overview\n\nA reliable task queue is a system that ensures work items (tasks, jobs, messages) are processed exactly once or at least once, survive system failures, and maintain ordering guarantees where needed. The core challenge is balancing durability, performance, and complexity.\n\n## Core Design Principles\n\n### 1. Durability: Never Lose a Task\n\nThe fundamental rule is that a task must not be lost between the moment a producer enqueues it and the moment a consumer has successfully processed it (and acknowledged it). This requires:\n\n**Write-ahead logging (WAL):** Before acknowledging a task to the producer, persist it to durable storage. The typical flow:\n\n```\nProducer \u2192 [Append to WAL] \u2192 [Acknowledge to Producer] \u2192 [Schedule for delivery]\n```\n\nIf the system crashes after the WAL append but before delivery, the task is replayed on restart. If it crashes before the WAL append, the producer must retry (the producer should treat non-acknowledged sends as failures).\n\n**Storage options:**\n- `fsync`'d file (simplest, sufficient for many use cases)\n- Database table (PostgreSQL, SQLite)\n- Durable log (Kafka, write-behind cache)\n\nFor a single-node implementation, an append-only file with `fsync` after each write is the most straightforward approach. For",
